{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2408.00821, what challenges exist in defining regulated units for approval in frontier AI, and how does this compare to biopharmaceuticals?", "answer": "Frontier AI lacks clear risk metrics and faces challenges from system modifications, unlike biopharmaceuticals where regulated units are well-defined by law.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "First, there are not clear metrics to characterize the risk posed by AI systems, in part due to their generality and complexity."}, {"page": 3, "quote": "approval regulation in biopharmaceuticals generally possesses an identifiable object of regulation. This is not exogenous to regulation but is defined in part by the law itself, in the concepts of Investigational New Drug and New Molecular Entity or New Therapeutic Biological Products, or in the case of medical devices, Class III devices."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2408.00821, how does the industrial structure affect the feasibility of approval regulation for both biopharmaceuticals and AI?", "answer": "An oligopolistic structure facilitates approval regulation in biopharma, but AI's structure is less clear and may not support similar oversight.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "there may be reason to believe that the feasibility of approval regulation depends in part upon an oligopolistic industrial structure."}, {"page": 5, "quote": "there is far less known about the industrial structure of the AI industry. This fact stems in part from the novelty of the industry and its rapid rise, but also from the fact of its nonregulation."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2408.00821, what does 'approval regulation' mean in the context of FDA-style regimes?", "answer": "Approval regulation is when a regulator requires firms to test products before further activities, using test data to decide on approval.", "evidence": [{"page": 2, "quote": "approval regulation... as a regime in which a regulator requires a firm to engage in testing before conducting subsequent activities (e.g. releasing a product), and in which this testing generates data that is used by the regulator to decide whether part or all of the subsequent activities can be conducted."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2408.00821, what are the main categories considered in Table 1 for assessing approval regulation in AI?", "answer": "The main categories are Scope, Observability, and Enforceability.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "| Category       | Considerations                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |"}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2408.00821, what is one reason why AI red-teaming may not reveal all possible risks?", "answer": "Red-teaming is biased toward scenarios humans have already imagined, missing unforeseen risks.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "However, red-teaming shares a property of stress testing that tests are biased towards studying scenarios that humans have already imagined."}]} ,
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2408.00821, what is a challenge for regulators in monitoring unreported AI model development?", "answer": "Unreported model development may be detected via compute usage, but many small labs or foreign actors complicate detection.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "foundation model development that is not reported by developers may be observable to compute providers because of the scale of frontier AI model training runs (Sastry et al. 2024)."}]}
]}
